Exactly: or selects a branch; and builds a larger state space.

“Or” asks which world survives.
“And” asks whether the worlds can coexist, alternate, nest, or transform one another.

The trick is that and must preserve distinctions. Otherwise synthesis becomes confusion. Hold both long enough to discover the higher-order pattern—then use or only where reality imposes an actual constraint.

Have anchor and motion.
Sovereignty and relationship.
Mystery and checksum.
Climb and leave handles behind.